# kvmshare: physical input-device access (kernel input isolation).
#
# This file is the REFERENCE shape of the rule. The actual rule installed
# at /etc/udev/rules.d/99-kvmshare-input.rules is GENERATED by the
# installer (`kvmshare-install --input-access`, run automatically on every
# install/update and by `make install`) with the installing desktop user's
# numeric uid baked in as OWNER:
#
#   KERNEL=="event*", SUBSYSTEM=="input", OWNER="<uid>",
#       GROUP="<input gid or primary gid>", MODE="0660", TAG+="uaccess"
#
# Why ownership instead of group membership: supplementary groups only
# apply to processes started after the change (a running server keeps its
# old groups until re-login), while node ownership applies to every
# process immediately. OWNER is a plain udev property, so the rule works
# identically on udev and eudev, with or without logind; TAG+="uaccess"
# additionally lets a logind system grant the active seat user an ACL.
# The installer also chowns the live /dev/input/event* nodes on the spot,
# so a server that is already running gains access on its next rescan —
# no restart, no re-login, no user commands. Uninstall removes the rule.
